WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.936
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:04.370
you know from
films like "Booksmart"

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.772
and shows such as
"Unbelievable" and "Dopesick."

00:00:06.772 --> 00:00:08.374
She stars in "Rosaline,"

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:11.544
which is streaming on
Hulu and Disney+ October 14th.

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.779
She also stars in
"Ticket to Paradise,"

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:15.481
which is in theaters
October 21st.

00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:16.615
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:19.152
-Are you guys sure
you're good with this?

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.655
Because you know
you could just tell me.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:26.058
You don't have to do
a whole Trojan Horse thing.

00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:27.327
-What -- what -- what are you --
what are you --

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:29.529
-What? What's that?
-Hmm?

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.498
-Didn't you tell me
that story, Mom?

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:32.498
-No, I told you.

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:33.633
Trojan --
I told you that story.

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.067
That was me.
-It doesn't matter.

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:38.238
Doesn't matter.
We just are both here for you.

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:39.905
-Yeah, lockstep.

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:43.876
-Please welcome back to the show
Kaitlyn Dever, everybody!

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:46.079
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:58.390 --> 00:00:59.725
-She's amazing.
-Welcome back, Kaitlyn.

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:00.760
-She's so cool.
-Isn't she great?

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.794
We're very lucky.

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:03.930
We're very lucky
to have her this week.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.966
I'm wondering -- You've done
such wonderful work

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:09.468
in recent years in things
like "Unbelievable,"

00:01:09.468 --> 00:01:10.870
in things like "Dopesick."

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.306
They're a little heavy.
-Little bit, yeah.

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:16.142
-And I'm wondering if
it's nice for you

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:18.711
to get to do something light,
like romantic comedy

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:20.713
or a Shakespearean
period comedy?

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:24.283
-Yes. You know, it really is.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.953
I'm so fortunate to be able to

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.555
kind of jump around
between the two.

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:33.059
I've never been able to choose
between comedy or drama.

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:34.393
So, it's nice, because it is

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:35.961
still a challenge,
though, comedy.

00:01:35.961 --> 00:01:39.665
And it does work a different
part of your brain in a way.

00:01:39.665 --> 00:01:43.136
But I've had the best time
on these rom-coms.

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:44.804
It is really relaxing.
I feel relaxed.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.706
-I would imagine.

00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:49.008
I guess it could
break either way.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.844
Is it very relaxing to be
doing a scene with

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:53.245
George Clooney
and Julia Roberts,

00:01:53.245 --> 00:01:55.514
who maybe
do it better than anyone,

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:58.217
especially in the genre,
or is it the stress

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:00.520
of knowing that
you're doing it with two of

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.321
the people that
are the best of the genre?

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:04.891
-It does -- You know, I thought
it would kind of level out,

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:06.725
and then it kind of
just goes up and down,

00:02:06.725 --> 00:02:08.761
that, like, nervousness
and fear like, "Oh, my God.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:09.895
I'm in a movie with

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:11.197
George Clooney
and Julia Roberts."

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.732
And then it kind of
chills out again.

00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:15.702
But they -- The two of them
are just the best.

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:17.270
I mean, they really --
Their friendship,

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.739
being around that kind of energy
is just really so sweet.

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:22.775
-It's also great that --
I mean, again,

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.677
you play their daughter
in this film,

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:28.748
so you're allowed to walk around
the rest of your life and say,

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:30.583
"I know what you're thinking.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:32.986
Did George Clooney and
Julia Roberts have a baby?"

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.523
-Listen, it's true.
-Yeah.

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:39.459
-I got to do that.
-Yeah.

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:40.926
-It's true.

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:43.429
-There are some really wonderful
outtakes at end of the film

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:46.199
that make it clear that you got
to ad lib, as well.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:47.500
-Yeah.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.770
-I can't imagine what the energy
of that would be like.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.839
-I mean, the energy on the set
was so much fun.

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:57.543
I can't even tell you.

00:02:57.543 --> 00:02:59.679
George and Julia were
goofing around all the time.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.213
They love making
each other laugh.

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:04.617
They love making everyone around
on set laugh, me laugh.

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:07.219
It was hard to keep a
straight face most of the time.

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:08.521
And it was funny.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:11.758
I really think about,
like, how I just normalized

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:14.026
taking a speedboat
to work every day.

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:16.128
-Yeah. We should note,
on top of everything else,

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:17.463
you are shooting on an island.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:18.797
-Right.

00:03:18.797 --> 00:03:22.335
-So I guess you're --
Ultimately, you'll do movies.

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:23.636
You're willing to
make sacrifices.

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.137
You'll work on an island.
-Right, exactly. Exactly.

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:27.140
-You'll commute on a speedboat
and you'll work with

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.174
George Clooney
and Julia Roberts.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.442
-I guess.
-You'll do all those things.

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:30.509
-Yeah.
-For your art.

00:03:30.509 --> 00:03:32.745
-Right. It's for the art.

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:33.913
It's for the craft.

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:37.650
-Now, you also --
We mentioned you're --

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.019
So, this is a very --
a novel idea for a film.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:47.359
So, you are Rosaline,
who is Romeo's, basically, ex.

00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:48.461
-Yep. I was there first.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:51.197
-Nobody thinks about
what happened to

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:52.998
Romeo's ex before he met Juliet.

00:03:52.998 --> 00:03:55.735
Like, his life started then.
But we all know.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:56.936
-We all know.
-Yeah.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.137
-We all know.

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:00.139
It's such a cool idea, right?

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.941
It was something I read,
actually,

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:05.077
when I was like 15, this script.

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:08.147
And Rosaline is mentioned in
the original story,

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.783
"Romeo and Juliet," but we don't
know much about her.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.218
We don't get to
discover that much.

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:15.387
So it was really cool
to be able to, like, dive in

00:04:15.387 --> 00:04:18.124
and kind of --
With this thing, I mean,

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:19.525
it was modernized dialogue.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:20.960
It was super-funny.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.929
But you can really kind of do
anything and everything

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:23.963
with this kind of role.

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.832
So it was a lot of fun.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:29.668
-Do you feel -- Did you feel at
all constrained by the costumes

00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:32.137
or do you feel like it
brought you alive?

00:04:32.137 --> 00:04:37.110
-You know, well, we were
shooting in the middle of Italy

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.145
and in Rome and a
couple places outside of Rome --

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:41.514
-You have so -- I'm so glad

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:43.449
things are going like
this for you.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.817
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.218
Like, just --
-I was in Tuscany.

00:04:46.218 --> 00:04:47.653
-Because I do want to stress,

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:50.155
"Dopesick" was one of
my favorite things last year.

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:52.825
If you saw you in "Dopesick,"
you would never think

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:55.795
that you were going to be
in Italy,

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.263
you were going to be
on a beautiful island,

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:58.998
so I'm very happy
this has all happened for you.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:01.600
-Thank you. No, I'm so lucky.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.236
This movie
was really fun to make.

00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:06.105
But, yeah, those costumes
were -- I was wearing, like --

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:09.242
My wig was like 15 pounds,
and it was really hot.

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:12.044
And my costumes
basically felt like

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:13.379
I was just wearing a floor rug.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.314
But our costume designer
is a genius,

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:16.782
and these costumes
were absolutely beautiful,

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:17.816
every single one of them.

00:05:17.816 --> 00:05:19.718
But, yeah, it was hot.
It was hot.

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:23.889
-Did I read this correctly,
that you used your time in Italy

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:27.493
to learn how to drive a manual?

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:30.296
-Yeah, well, that's a
really strong statement,

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:33.832
because I don't think
I really succeeded there,

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.368
because --

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.437
-To even take it on
is pretty intense.

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:40.272
-Well, they gave me
a rental car.

00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:44.610
I got a rental car, and it just
happened to be a stick shift.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.478
And I was like, "I can do this."

00:05:46.478 --> 00:05:48.147
My mom knows how to drive.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:50.650
And her whole thing was,
"Clutch, gas.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:51.951
Clutch, gas."
-Yeah.

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:54.553
-She was just doing this
in the lesson the whole time.

00:05:54.553 --> 00:05:56.389
And I was like, "What is that?"
-Yeah.

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.257
That's what my dad did, as well.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:00.226
And I wanted to say,
"To begin with,

00:06:00.226 --> 00:06:01.493
you don't use your hands,
so stop."

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:02.961
-Right. Yeah.

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:04.096
And there's three of them
down there.

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:06.132
I can't do it.

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:08.600
But I decided, you know,
my sister -- My mom left us.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:12.371
And my sister and I wanted to go
up into this, like, really cool,

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:13.939
like, town that had been there
since like the 12th century.

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:15.708
-Did she leave you
because of how mean you were

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:17.009
about the clutch-gas thing or...

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:19.579
-No, she went on an Italian
adventure with my dad.

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:21.214
-Alright. Very nice.
-Yes.

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:24.183
But I decided to take
this little trip.

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:27.487
And I think I got --
I was like, "I got this."

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:29.288
And there was a nice -- Like,
right before we got up this,

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:32.024
like, tiny, tiny hill --
It was, like, tiny.

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:35.128
But, like, there was, like,
this nice parking lot.

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:36.895
We could have stopped there.

00:06:36.895 --> 00:06:39.398
And I was like, "Let's just get
where we're going.

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:41.466
Let's just do that."

00:06:41.466 --> 00:06:43.169
And I literally --
Like, our car --

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:45.571
I was not in the right gear.
It was so bad.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:49.108
I was, like, rolling
backwards down the hill.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:50.910
It was the scariest thing.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:54.146
I'm just with my sister.
My sister and I never fight.

00:06:54.146 --> 00:06:56.315
I was screaming at her.

00:06:56.315 --> 00:06:57.883
I was furious.

00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.185
And she did nothing wrong,

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:01.854
but I was
blaming everything on her.

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:03.890
-Well, based on
my experiences in Italy,

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:05.724
they're super-chill
when American tourists

00:07:05.724 --> 00:07:08.961
are rolling backwards
down a hill.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:11.130
They definitely
keep it to themselves

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:12.431
and don't talk with their hands.

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:15.367
-Yep. There's suddenly
children at play everywhere.

00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:18.470
All of the sudden.
I was stressed out.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:19.872
It was so bad.

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.975
-That is lovely to hear you that
you and your sister don't fight.

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:24.777
You guys also collaborate.
-We do.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:27.046
-You write and perform
music together.

00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:30.382
Is that something you have done
since you were super-young?

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:33.352
-I mean, we've always
loved making music together.

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:34.620
I think we started writing --

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.789
actually taking it
seriously and writing together

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:41.126
probably when I was like 19, 20.

00:07:41.126 --> 00:07:44.396
And then we started
taking it even more seriously.

00:07:44.396 --> 00:07:46.765
And we are now
coming out with an E.P.

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.334
-Very exciting.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:49.935
-Yeah.

00:07:49.935 --> 00:07:52.939
We've released
a couple of singles.

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:57.109
Like, we released, like, one of
our first singles pre-pandemic,

00:07:57.109 --> 00:07:59.177
and now we're ready to
release some stuff.

00:07:59.177 --> 00:08:02.047
-Do you have a band name?

00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:03.716
Do you consider
yourselves a band?

00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:05.484
I know it's the two of you.
-Yes, we are a band.

00:08:05.484 --> 00:08:06.985
It is the two of us.

00:08:06.985 --> 00:08:10.189
And for longest time,
we've had a name

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:12.057
for our band,
but no one can pronounce it.

00:08:12.057 --> 00:08:13.826
No one can say it.
-What was it before?

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:16.762
-It was -- Okay, so,
formerly known as Beulahbelle.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:18.030
-Okay, Beulahbelle.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.299
-And now we're just Devers.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:24.369
-Okay, so you,
like, messed around,

00:08:24.369 --> 00:08:25.538
you looked around,
and you're like,

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:26.839
"We're just going to use
our last name."

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.874
-"We're just gonna
just go straight to the basics,

00:08:28.874 --> 00:08:31.176
just get back to it,"
you know?

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:32.445
It makes more sense.

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.546
-Do you collaborate well
when you are being creative

00:08:34.546 --> 00:08:36.015
when you write songs together?

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:38.451
Do you feel like somebody has
to take the lead or do you --

00:08:38.451 --> 00:08:39.652
-You know,
it's actually really cool.

00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:43.221
I've -- We've actually
gotten to know each other

00:08:43.221 --> 00:08:45.557
in a really --
Like, we've gotten to know

00:08:45.557 --> 00:08:48.194
more about each other
through the writing process.

00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:50.096
I used to be, like --
We used to be, like,

00:08:50.096 --> 00:08:52.698
afraid to show each other
the songs we've written,

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:55.568
and now it's,
like, a really cool,

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:57.769
collaborative process
that I get to do with her,

00:08:57.769 --> 00:08:59.071
which is really special.

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:02.074
-And true story that you
recorded with Phoebe Bridgers?

00:09:02.074 --> 00:09:03.442
-True story.
-That's amazing.

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:05.710
-Yes. Our producer is --
-She's wonderful.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.847
-Yeah, she works with the same
producer that we use.

00:09:08.847 --> 00:09:11.250
And he called us up one day
and he was like,

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.051
"You want to sing on a
Phoebe Bridgers Christmas song?"

00:09:13.051 --> 00:09:14.487
So we sang, like,
"Silent Night,"

00:09:14.487 --> 00:09:16.321
and just randomly --
It was the best.

00:09:16.321 --> 00:09:17.489
It was so cool.
-That's great.

00:09:17.489 --> 00:09:19.525
Did they --
When she called and asked,

00:09:19.525 --> 00:09:21.460
was she asking for Beulahbelle

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:22.861
and did you have to
break the news to her

00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:23.929
that you were
no longer using that name?

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:24.963
-Basically.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:24.963 --> 00:09:26.198
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:26.198 --> 00:09:27.299
-But she still
went ahead with it,

00:09:27.299 --> 00:09:28.300
which I think
speaks to your talents.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:29.701
-Yes, yes, exactly.

00:09:29.701 --> 00:09:31.136
-It's always
so lovely to have you here.

00:09:31.136 --> 00:09:33.205
Congratulations on both films.

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:34.540
And thanks so much
for being here.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:36.141
You guys...
-Thanks for having me.

00:09:36.141 --> 00:09:37.943
-...that's Kaitlyn Dever.

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:41.213
"Rosaline" is streaming on
Hulu and Disney+ October 14th.

00:09:41.213 --> 00:09:43.749
And "Ticket to Paradise"
is in theaters October 21st.

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:45.217
We'll be right back.

